19
00:00:54,981 --> 00:00:58,769
Rym: The boos came to a climax.

20
00:00:58,789 --> 00:01:11,800
Rym: If you're not in the East Coast of America versus other places where sports happen, there aren't that many places where, like, the hometown fans will aggressively boo their own team.

21
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:14,288
Rym: But New York is one of those places.

22
00:01:14,328 --> 00:01:17,480
Rym: It might be the booing-est place to boo your own team.

23
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:28,620
Rym: Even when Hank Lundquist, one of the best goalies to ever play the game, who never won a cup because he played for the Rangers, despite being great, when he was interviewed about his time, got his jersey put up in the rafters.

24
00:01:29,181 --> 00:01:31,760
Rym: One of the questions was something about, like, what it's like to play at MSG.

25
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:39,610
Rym: And I want to say he said something to the effect of, "It is a challenging environment to play in with the Rangers fans at

26
00:01:39,631 --> 00:01:39,831
Rym: MSG.".

27
00:01:39,871 --> 00:01:44,589
Scott: Yeah, I mean, you know, you're always going to boo an opponent, right?

28
00:01:44,811 --> 00:01:46,359
Scott: But that's like a perfunctory boo.

29
00:01:47,161 --> 00:01:47,924
Scott: Right, right.

30
00:01:48,024 --> 00:01:52,601
Scott: But the point is, is that, like, yeah, you can't blame the opponent for doing their best, right?

31
00:01:52,822 --> 00:01:55,069
Scott: It's like, of course that's how they're going to be, right?

32
00:01:55,149 --> 00:01:59,143
Scott: It's like, if our team wins, good job.

33
00:01:59,364 --> 00:02:00,188
Scott: You played well.

34
00:02:00,288 --> 00:02:03,300
Scott: If our team is bad, we're not going to, like, boo the other team.

35
00:02:03,420 --> 00:02:03,802
Scott: It's like, what?

36
00:02:03,822 --> 00:02:05,451
Scott: Were they supposed to let us win?

37
00:02:05,652 --> 00:02:07,059
Scott: It's like, no, you boo your own team.

38
00:02:07,079 --> 00:02:07,945
Scott: Like, you guys suck.

39
00:02:08,005 --> 00:02:09,031
Scott: You didn't play good enough.

40
00:02:09,112 --> 00:02:09,293
Scott: Boo.

41
00:02:09,312 --> 00:02:10,360
Rym: Boo ourselves.

42
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:17,554
Rym: There's a scene in Boardwalk Empire that's actually really relevant to this where some guys, like, tried to rob an illegal liquor warehouse.

43
00:02:17,574 --> 00:02:20,226
Rym: They got caught by the mobsters because they were hired by some other mobsters.

44
00:02:20,647 --> 00:02:26,129
Rym: And one of them's tied up and the head mobster's talking to him and he's like, look, I'm not mad at you.

45
00:02:26,209 --> 00:02:26,851
Rym: You're a thief.

46
00:02:27,292 --> 00:02:28,316
Rym: You're doing your job.

47
00:02:28,898 --> 00:02:37,306
Rym: I'm mad at, and he turns to his own, like, Lieutenant Mobbot guy, this motherfucker, for not locking the door and for letting you get into the first place.

48
00:02:39,093 --> 00:02:42,506
Rym: But then at the end, he also shoots the thief because you still got to try to win.

49
00:02:43,652 --> 00:02:51,325
Scott: Yeah, but, you know, the point being, there's a lot of places on Earth where people just cheer for their own team no matter how well or poorly they do.

50
00:02:51,385 --> 00:02:54,154
Scott: If anything, ideas of good sportsmanship.

51
00:02:54,274 --> 00:03:01,061
Scott: And if that's, if it's Little League, if it's, you know, something that's not, you know, it's like, of course you would cheer, right?

52
00:03:01,402 --> 00:03:04,835
Scott: You know, and there's other occasions where you might cheer a loser, right?

53
00:03:05,036 --> 00:03:06,100
Scott: For good reasons.

54
00:03:07,481 --> 00:03:20,840
Scott: But if the, in a professional sport that you're, especially if you're paying to see and your team that you support isn't doing well because, and it's their fault that they're not doing well.

55
00:03:20,981 --> 00:03:23,959
Rym: And it is definitely the Rangers' fault that they're not doing well right now.

56
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:24,601
Scott: Right.

57
00:03:24,661 --> 00:03:27,395
Scott: You can't, these places that just always cheer their own team.

58
00:03:27,456 --> 00:03:28,601
Scott: It's like, seriously, people.

59
00:03:28,681 --> 00:03:35,083
Rym: To quote a coworker today who walked up to my desk, he just said, he comes, he walks by and he literally just says,

60
00:03:35,806 --> 00:03:36,508
Rym: "Shameful.".

61
00:03:36,809 --> 00:03:39,057
Rym: And he shakes his head and he says, "Just

62
00:03:39,117 --> 00:03:39,639
Rym: shameful.".

63
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:40,845
Rym: And I said, "I was

64
00:03:40,905 --> 00:03:41,187
Rym: there.".

65
00:03:41,287 --> 00:03:42,494
Rym: And he said, "I'm so

66
00:03:42,655 --> 00:03:43,078
Rym: sorry.".

67
00:03:43,890 --> 00:03:44,320
Scott: That's right.

68
00:03:46,902 --> 00:03:49,760
Scott: No matter how bad I do it, my, see if, you know, if I do it my job.

69
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:50,041
Rym: Yeah.

70
00:03:50,101 --> 00:03:53,012
Rym: Nobody has ever booed me for fucking up at work.

71
00:03:53,995 --> 00:03:55,019
Rym: Actually, no, I take that back.

72
00:03:55,059 --> 00:03:55,561
Rym: I got booed.

73
00:03:55,942 --> 00:04:02,422
Rym: We used to, when I worked at IBM, we had a star, like an inverse trophy that we'd pass around.

74
00:04:02,682 --> 00:04:08,963
Rym: Whoever had fucked up the most egregiously most recently, and you would get gently booed as it was handed to you.

75
00:04:10,989 --> 00:04:11,952
Rym: I do miss that kind of thing.

76
00:04:11,993 --> 00:04:16,827
Rym: That was, I held, I held the trophy a couple of times, but I tried not to hold it for very long.

77
00:04:17,310 --> 00:04:27,988
Rym: So, in some news, as you might know, there's, there's a lot of games, not a lot, but there's several games that have tried to effectively clone Overwatch in one way or another.

78
00:04:28,048 --> 00:04:32,485
Rym: Like, Overwatch kind of is a template for a kind of competitive FPS.

79
00:04:33,047 --> 00:04:36,902
Scott: I mean, that kind of, Overwatch isn't the first of those games either, right?

80
00:04:37,082 --> 00:04:50,922
Rym: Oh, absolutely not, but Overwatch sort of refined it to, like, a certain sub-template of a certain, like, set of characteristics, and I don't want, I don't want to get into, like, a whole thing about what I think those characteristics are, because it'll, this will turn into the Overwatch show.

81
00:04:50,942 --> 00:04:53,292
Scott: It became the most prominent of those games.

82
00:04:53,393 --> 00:05:01,381
Rym: Yeah, but it has the characteristics of, it's a team-based competitive FPS with different maps that have different kinds of objectives.

83
00:05:01,782 --> 00:05:04,329
Rym: It's extremely focused on skill.

84
00:05:05,371 --> 00:05:07,657
Rym: It is not in any way pay-to-win.

85
00:05:08,018 --> 00:05:10,426
Rym: There's pay-to-do stuff, but there's no pay-to-win.

86
00:05:10,506 --> 00:05:11,450
Rym: Like, you get everything.

87
00:05:11,852 --> 00:05:16,148
Rym: Nothing stops you from using the thing, the tools at your disposal to try to win.

88
00:05:16,168 --> 00:05:17,653
Rym: It has good matchmaking.

89
00:05:17,994 --> 00:05:20,622
Rym: It has positions like a sports team.

90
00:05:21,003 --> 00:05:34,040
Rym: It has the trifecta in an FPS of tank, DPS, and support, but Marvel Arrivals is noteworthy for, uh, everyone said, "Oh, it's kind of an Overwatch clone, just with the Marvel IP,".

91
00:05:34,281 --> 00:05:52,538
Rym: and I assumed it had at least a few other things going on, and it wasn't just a carbon copy, but having actually played it now, the degree to which it is just a copy-paste of Overwatch with Marvel IP and more randomness surprised me, despite expecting exactly that.

92
00:05:53,060 --> 00:05:54,165
Rym: Like, some of the characters...

93
00:05:54,426 --> 00:05:57,903
Scott: Every card game out there, how many card games out there just copy magic, right?

94
00:05:57,943 --> 00:06:18,055
Scott: It's like, you know, it's like, hey, you know, it takes a long time and investment of, you know, money, resources to build a game up, and you learn lessons, and so if you want to compete with a thing that exists for the same users, the same players, the same customers, it's like, you have to take from the lessons that they've already learned.

95
00:06:18,175 --> 00:06:20,827
Scott: If you start from scratch, you will never catch up to them.

96
00:06:20,887 --> 00:06:24,662
Rym: And I want to be clear, I don't think this is bad, nor am I blaming them.

97
00:06:24,722 --> 00:06:25,907
Rym: It's the smart thing to do.

98
00:06:26,148 --> 00:06:33,713
Rym: There weren't a million clones of Jihad, the card game, or SimCity, the card game, that everyone cloned Magic, the card game.

99
00:06:33,773 --> 00:06:34,054
Rym: Right.

100
00:06:34,415 --> 00:06:44,044
Scott: It's like, in any business, if you're coming in late, you have to look at the champ, copy the champ, just to even be in the game.

101
00:06:44,065 --> 00:06:50,488
Scott: You'll still be behind them, right, because you won't have the staff knowledge, and then you have no choice but to do that.

102
00:06:50,568 --> 00:07:03,775
Scott: So, they decided they wanted to compete instead of making a brand new game that's a new kind of game or something that doesn't compete with anything, and I guess that's, you know, seen as less risky, right?

103
00:07:04,197 --> 00:07:11,378
Scott: Go with something people are familiar with, and, you know, sell it with the IP, and you can see Sony.

104
00:07:11,558 --> 00:07:44,740
Scott: They probably, you know, I haven't played the Sony Concord, which is dead now, right, which cost a zillion dollars, and I haven't played Overwatch or Marvel Rivals either, but I would suspect that the Sony Concord thing that bombed was maybe a better game, maybe even better than Overwatch, probably better than the Marvel Rivals, but it didn't get anyone to play it because no one gave a shit, and the Marvel Rivals, it's probably the worst of the three games, just based on the little information I know, because it has the Marvel IP, is probably gonna come out ahead here.

105
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:45,120
Rym: Yep.

106
00:07:45,281 --> 00:08:08,670
Rym: So, as someone who was in on Overwatch from day zero, because it was based on everything I like about team-based FPSs, and there's a lot going on there, but I still play Overwatch daily, but it's kind of my current lifestyle game, even though I've had a weird urge to play Counter-Strike again, because I saw that they remade Train, and part of my brain rumbled and wanted me to play, but anyway, no worthy things.

107
00:08:08,710 --> 00:08:18,368
Rym: having played it now, and this is not a review, I haven't played this game enough, and there's a big caveat to anything I'm about to say, as Scott and I were discussing before the show, one, this game just came out.

108
00:08:18,830 --> 00:08:23,663
Rym: Like, imagine comparing the rules of baseball in the year 2024 to the rules of baseball in the year 1904.

109
00:08:23,663 --> 00:08:52,404
Rym: So there's a lot, like this game is lacking a lot of polish, the matchmaking doesn't know anything about the player base yet, like there's a lot of factors here, but I will say number one, my first impression, I didn't read tutorial, I didn't play a tutorial, I read nothing, I went into the game, I changed my mouse to inverted, I mapped my keys just based on what I thought things did, and I played, picked a random character,

110
00:08:52,424 --> 00:08:56,140
Scott: and no tutorial, they forced you to go through anything?

111
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:59,579
Rym: No, you can do the tutorial, I skipped it all, jumped right into a match with real people.

112
00:09:00,401 --> 00:09:01,304
Scott: Okay.

113
00:09:01,324 --> 00:09:21,215
Rym: And despite knowing nothing, if you've ever played Overwatch even once, you're fine, it's just Overwatch, like everything about it, just Overwatch, with minor surprising differences, like the characters all have more powers than Overwatch, which the effect is, the game is just way more chaotic because there's more crazy shit happening everywhere.

114
00:09:21,235 --> 00:09:21,920
Rym: that's kind of random.

115
00:09:22,980 --> 00:09:49,346
Rym: Two, your ultimates, like when you charge an ultimate, like your big power and use it, Overwatch, the matches or the rounds in a game are all discrete, but in this game, if you lose round one of a match, but you've got your ultimate charged up, you keep that charge in round two, which makes the game, again, a little more random, a little more close, a little more rubber banding.

116
00:09:49,366 --> 00:09:51,272
Scott: That's what fighting games do, right?

117
00:09:51,352 --> 00:09:54,361
Scott: If your meter carries over, right?

118
00:09:54,622 --> 00:10:01,653
Scott: So if you lose, you might lose round one, but you got your meter and they use their meter to beat you in round one, and now you're coming into round two with a full meter.

119
00:10:02,195 --> 00:10:03,801
Scott: That kind of makes sense.

120
00:10:03,902 --> 00:10:12,797
Rym: And again, I'm not saying it's bad, but it is different because the way it works in Overwatch, which makes a very different feel, is like you're playing a best of three.

121
00:10:13,238 --> 00:10:20,306
Rym: It's more like a hockey championship best of three, where whoever wins the first one, everything resets, clean slate, go to a new map.

122
00:10:20,386 --> 00:10:24,803
Rym: Now you're playing match two, like that's how a match of Overwatch works in effect.

123
00:10:25,064 --> 00:10:26,930
Scott: I mean, Counter Strike, you keep your money, your guns.

124
00:10:27,632 --> 00:10:27,833
Rym: Yep.

125
00:10:28,555 --> 00:10:34,140
Rym: So, but the thing that stood out was jumping in, not knowing the characters, picking a random one and just playing.

126
00:10:35,682 --> 00:10:39,959
Rym: I don't think I died for my first three matches before some kid finally killed me.

127
00:10:40,721 --> 00:10:46,162
Rym: It's real easy to click on heads because there's like no scatter, like everything's kind of just hit scan-y.

128
00:10:46,664 --> 00:10:57,407
Rym: The game feels, at least at this time, to me playing with the standard matchmaking extremely easy.

129
00:10:57,427 --> 00:10:58,489
Rym: And we'll see where that goes.

130
00:10:58,650 --> 00:11:12,712
Rym: I suspect due to just the random kind of broad nature of all the powers the characters have, I expect this game is in fact more random and does have a lower skill cap than games like Overwatch.

131
00:11:13,093 --> 00:11:14,538
Rym: And again, I'm not saying that's bad.

132
00:11:14,679 --> 00:11:16,445
Rym: It's just different from a game like that.

133
00:11:17,327 --> 00:11:29,044
Rym: I suspect the competitive scene is going to end up being more Fortnite and less like the more seriously competitive games that are more skill-based and less random.

134
00:11:29,887 --> 00:11:31,012
Rym: And otherwise, I don't know.

135
00:11:31,113 --> 00:11:41,393
Rym: I'm going to play it more, get a feel of it, but I'm going to give it the old college try to try to give it a deep review about a month from now and try to do a serious comparison of it to other FPSs.

136
00:11:41,413 --> 00:11:44,982
Scott: Do they, I know it's on PC, Steam.

137
00:11:45,042 --> 00:11:45,745
Scott: It's just on Steam.

138
00:11:45,786 --> 00:11:46,629
Rym: I just right-clicked.

139
00:11:46,770 --> 00:11:48,538
Scott: It's also on the PS5 and Xbox.

140
00:11:48,558 --> 00:11:50,164
Scott: Do they let there be cross-play or no?

141
00:11:50,586 --> 00:11:51,871
Rym: I don't know, actually.

142
00:11:52,553 --> 00:11:53,517
Rym: Didn't ask me anything.

143
00:11:53,597 --> 00:11:55,323
Rym: I could have set up a controller, but I didn't.

144
