354
00:33:08,921 --> 00:33:15,740
Scott: And you've got your whole Reading Terminal Market there, which is full of amazing places to eat.

355
00:33:16,182 --> 00:33:31,415
Scott: The problem is that the capacity of these places is not only limited to the Pax attendees, but it's, I don't know why, that on five o'clock- Reading Terminal Market was more crowded than the convention around lunchtime on Saturday.

356
00:33:32,178 --> 00:33:37,015
Scott: Right, it's like at five o'clock these restaurants are filling up with locals and shit.

357
00:33:37,055 --> 00:33:38,945
Scott: It's like, "Whoa, why are you people eating so early?

358
00:33:38,965 --> 00:33:39,749
Scott: What the hell is going

359
00:33:39,810 --> 00:33:39,850
Scott: on?".

360
00:33:39,890 --> 00:33:42,280
Scott: I'm used to New York where people are eating at seven, eight, nine.

361
00:33:43,184 --> 00:33:51,678
Scott: So it's like you're trying to eat, your enforcer shift is going to start at six thirty or your concert is going to start six seven for the night events.

362
00:33:52,059 --> 00:33:54,870
Scott: You want to eat at four or five, which is normally early.

363
00:33:55,372 --> 00:33:58,884
Scott: And anywhere you go that's close is jam-packed of attendees.

364
00:33:58,945 --> 00:34:04,843
Scott: And anywhere that you go that's far is a little farther is full of residents.

365
00:34:05,164 --> 00:34:08,255
Scott: And anything further away is too far away, but you won't make it back.

366
00:34:08,597 --> 00:34:18,630
Rym: And for various reasons in Philly, uh, there are like, so again- You gotta eat the chicken fingers and pretzel in the convention center?

367
00:34:18,650 --> 00:34:21,870
Rym: I did eat the convention center pretzel on two non-consecutive occasions this year.

368
00:34:22,290 --> 00:34:22,411
Scott: Two?

369
00:34:22,571 --> 00:34:23,373
Scott: I only ate one pretzel.

370
00:34:24,177 --> 00:34:27,630
Rym: I paid the extra two dollars to get the pile of mustard to go on it.

371
00:34:27,870 --> 00:34:28,391
Scott: I did not.

372
00:34:28,452 --> 00:34:29,134
Scott: I just ate a pretzel.

373
00:34:29,775 --> 00:34:42,136
Rym: But I will, shamefully, because we live in a capitalist society, the way Scott and I solve that at other conventions we go to is we eat at the really expensive places because they won't be full.

374
00:34:43,981 --> 00:34:47,511
Rym: By fax unplugged, the expensive places are also all full.

375
00:34:47,772 --> 00:34:48,956
Rym: Like, money doesn't save you.

376
00:34:50,201 --> 00:34:59,678
Rym: And even worse, trying to get reservations, a lot of places in that area, one, will not take a reservation larger than six, which, sure, like, I can understand why they wouldn't.

377
00:35:00,020 --> 00:35:02,113
Scott: Well, they're also- all these restaurants are like small.

378
00:35:02,153 --> 00:35:04,545
Scott: There's not- there aren't places that are like big ballrooms.

379
00:35:04,586 --> 00:35:06,696
Scott: They're all like, you know, small little places.

380
00:35:06,716 --> 00:35:13,066
Rym: I got walking home, you know, at the end of the day on Sunday when I stopped to get dinner, and I walked pretty far away from the convention center.

381
00:35:13,427 --> 00:35:14,833
Rym: I was halfway to the train station.

382
00:35:14,853 --> 00:35:15,736
Rym: I went to a restaurant.

383
00:35:16,177 --> 00:35:21,375
Rym: By myself, I walk in and they weren't sure if they could seat one person.

384
00:35:22,077 --> 00:35:22,539
Scott: At the bar?

385
00:35:22,940 --> 00:35:27,596
Rym: I sat at the bar and ate my full dinner sitting at the bar with no empty seats around me.

386
00:35:27,616 --> 00:35:28,699
Rym: I've done that.

387
00:35:28,940 --> 00:35:32,672
Rym: I would say 100% of the people in this restaurant were from PAX.

388
00:35:34,779 --> 00:35:35,441
Scott: I used the trolley.

389
00:35:35,461 --> 00:35:36,344
Scott: He just went to the train station.

390
00:35:36,524 --> 00:35:36,725
Rym: Yep.

391
00:35:37,548 --> 00:35:42,368
Rym: But I did find some restaurants that I think we could get a reservation at next year that are around there.

392
00:35:42,388 --> 00:35:54,570
Rym: Anyway, one last observation on the restaurant situation that I- is something I noticed about Philly, which has broader societal implications, but I think it's worth talking about.

393
00:35:54,992 --> 00:35:56,642
Rym: Philly is a very poor city in America.

394
00:35:56,662 --> 00:36:00,259
Rym: Like, if you're not familiar with the East Coast, Philadelphia is a huge city.

395
00:36:00,280 --> 00:36:07,510
Rym: It's one of the biggest cities in North America, but it's also per capita one of the poorest cities in North America for a lot of reasons.

396
00:36:08,670 --> 00:36:16,300
Rym: It's very much a working person city, and something I notice at this Unplugged, and I feel like it's happened before, but like I noticed it this time.

397
00:36:17,044 --> 00:36:25,163
Rym: When you go to New York or Boston or any other city and you talk to wait staff at restaurants, fancy ones, not so fancy ones, they kind of just talk to you like a normal person would.

398
00:36:25,686 --> 00:36:32,887
Rym: But in Philly, almost every restaurant we went to used the restaurant lingo talking to customers.

399
00:36:33,529 --> 00:36:37,688
Rym: Like, three different restaurants I went to, which they're like, "Oh hey, what do you need?

400
00:36:37,728 --> 00:36:38,010
Rym: A four

401
00:36:38,090 --> 00:36:38,412
Rym: top?".

402
00:36:38,894 --> 00:36:42,550
Scott: Like, that's not something- I didn't hear- Nobody said anything like that to me.

403
00:36:42,630 --> 00:36:49,450
Rym: Oh, Scott, the place we ate at with everybody asked if we needed a four top or a six top.

404
00:36:49,711 --> 00:36:56,958
Rym: The same waitress said she couldn't get into Reading Terminal to get breakfast because it was full and asked us what the fuck this convention was.

405
00:36:57,982 --> 00:37:04,164
Rym: The diner downstairs also literally said, "We don't serve high tops or one

406
00:37:04,204 --> 00:37:04,505
Rym: tops.".

407
00:37:04,686 --> 00:37:07,074
Rym: She said that when I walked in, and I said, "No, I just want

408
00:37:07,094 --> 00:37:07,575
Rym: takeaway.".

409
00:37:08,579 --> 00:37:17,790
Rym: I've just noticed the wait staff use lingo that usually only wait staff use with each other in restaurants, and it's just something I noted.

410
00:37:17,850 --> 00:37:22,176
Rym: I'm going to try to pay more attention, see if I hear that lingo more in New York, and I'm just not noticing it.

411
00:37:22,518 --> 00:37:23,523
Rym: Maybe it's a Philly thing.

412
00:37:23,543 --> 00:37:24,247
Rym: I don't know.

413
00:37:26,151 --> 00:37:32,290
Scott: I think it's more like a confirmation bias, like you heard one thing two times, like when you saw a bear and then you saw another bear.

414
00:37:32,930 --> 00:37:37,393
Rym: But I've only seen one bear in the wild, so I haven't seen the second bear, despite looking hard.

415
00:37:38,324 --> 00:37:38,627
Scott: Sure.

416
00:37:39,790 --> 00:37:41,054
Rym: But Paxson plugged itself.

417
00:37:41,736 --> 00:37:43,842
Rym: Honestly, so big changes.

418
00:37:44,103 --> 00:37:47,513
Rym: One, that there is a bunch of tabletop in the main area.

419
00:37:48,036 --> 00:37:49,923
Rym: They finally did the thing that they needed to do.

420
00:37:50,385 --> 00:37:53,778
Rym: Tabletop free play was like the majority of the space in the convention center.

421
00:37:54,581 --> 00:37:58,074
Scott: Well, I don't know if it was actually more square footage or not.

422
00:37:58,114 --> 00:38:00,463
Scott: Maybe it was a little more, but they moved.

423
00:38:00,884 --> 00:38:08,134
Scott: They took an upstairs giant ballroom and they put the first look up there and they put some more free play space up there, which is good for two reasons.

424
00:38:08,495 --> 00:38:12,830
Scott: Number one, because that was a place that you had to know to go.

425
00:38:13,513 --> 00:38:17,835
Scott: They tried to make it obvious with signage, but, you know, I don't believe in signs and signs don't work that well.

426
00:38:18,277 --> 00:38:25,550
Rym: Oh, I saw hordes of people looking for tables in the loud, uncomfortable main area, despite the fact that there were plenty of open tables upstairs.

427
00:38:26,410 --> 00:38:27,112
Scott: Yeah, exactly.

428
00:38:27,172 --> 00:38:35,456
Scott: But that upstairs area was not only had some tables available, depending on the time, but was a carpeted area.

429
00:38:35,837 --> 00:38:43,162
Scott: So you could actually not ruin your hearing by being in that area, whereas the main hall is full of concrete and will destroy you.

430
00:38:43,584 --> 00:38:47,696
Rym: I honestly cannot game in the main area.

431
00:38:47,736 --> 00:38:51,289
Rym: That roar is too much for me and I lose my voice too fast yelling over it.

432
00:38:52,130 --> 00:38:54,177
Scott: But they did also.

433
00:38:54,437 --> 00:38:57,487
Scott: this made the first look area was spread out more.

434
00:38:57,547 --> 00:39:01,360
Scott: Last year it was just a section in the in the main hall.

435
00:39:01,461 --> 00:39:04,150
Scott: And so it was cramped and you couldn't get a seat in there.

436
00:39:04,230 --> 00:39:05,034
Scott: Now it was spread out.

437
00:39:05,075 --> 00:39:06,864
Scott: They had more copies of more games.

438
00:39:06,904 --> 00:39:11,410
Scott: You could actually, you know, check some of them out and hear the person teaching you the game, whatever.

439
00:39:12,691 --> 00:39:22,004
Scott: But also because they had the upstairs area, they did actually reduce the free, you know, the free play was squished and the expo hall part was expanded.

440
00:39:22,064 --> 00:39:22,185
Scott: Right.

441
00:39:22,265 --> 00:39:32,174
Scott: It was more exposed space in the big main room that pushed farther, which meant more exhibitors, more smaller exhibitors, more smaller exhibitors.

442
00:39:32,194 --> 00:39:43,129
Rym: I was about to call out because there was a good healthy diversity of booths like with a wide range of really, really indie stuff, a lot of which I'd never heard of.

443
00:39:44,131 --> 00:39:44,292
Scott: Right.

444
00:39:44,332 --> 00:39:47,180
Scott: You had all kinds of like.

445
00:39:47,341 --> 00:39:49,788
Scott: every aspect of tabletop stuff was covered.

446
00:39:49,828 --> 00:39:53,562
Scott: You had your, you know, your people selling D&D maps and stuff.

447
00:39:53,823 --> 00:39:55,048
Scott: You had people selling minis.

448
00:39:55,088 --> 00:39:57,902
Scott: You got people selling mini paintings, a lot of painting booths.

449
00:39:58,083 --> 00:39:59,370
Scott: There were some good ones, actually.

450
00:39:59,811 --> 00:40:01,960
Scott: There was a lot of indie art, not just the usual.

451
00:40:02,000 --> 00:40:07,159
Scott: You have one indie RPG booth, no several indie RPG booths going on with different stuff.

452
00:40:07,199 --> 00:40:07,379
Rym: Yep.

453
00:40:07,460 --> 00:40:14,563
Rym: I didn't buy every crow themed solo RPG in the library, in the expo hall, but I bought, I think the majority of them.

454
00:40:15,747 --> 00:40:15,948
Scott: Right.

455
00:40:15,968 --> 00:40:20,641
Scott: You had, you know, the big tabletop publishers, your ASMO days were there.

456
00:40:20,661 --> 00:40:21,183
Scott: Right.

457
00:40:21,584 --> 00:40:24,133
Scott: But also the smaller publishers.

458
00:40:24,433 --> 00:40:24,714
Scott: Right.

459
00:40:25,015 --> 00:40:32,297
Scott: Little, you know, your Oink games and even smaller publishers than that with one game or two games that they had.

460
00:40:32,317 --> 00:40:44,241
Scott: Just basically every aspect of the tabletop industry, almost, you know, except for the lack of the Chessex booth, there were still plenty of people selling dice, just not Chessex.

461
00:40:44,562 --> 00:40:49,680
Scott: It's like, if there's a something tabletop that you were interested in, it was there somewhere.

462
00:40:49,961 --> 00:40:59,677
Scott: What I found was interesting was the proportions in the expo area were different from the proportions of the content of the con, which some of it is just by necessity.

463
00:40:59,737 --> 00:40:59,918
Scott: Right.

464
00:40:59,958 --> 00:41:10,149
Scott: It's like, if you, it's like there was so much in indie RPG and RPG stuff in the expo hall, but the con, they got an RPG area, right?

465
00:41:10,190 --> 00:41:15,812
Scott: There's plenty of RPG going on, but it's like, it's limited based on the space available, the tables available.

466
00:41:15,832 --> 00:41:20,788
Scott: A lot of those games need GMs organizations, several hours of time.

467
00:41:20,808 --> 00:41:21,350
Scott: Right.

468
00:41:21,470 --> 00:41:25,130
Scott: Which means only so many, even if you have a table and a GM, it's like, well, there you go.

469
00:41:25,210 --> 00:41:26,475
Scott: That's four hours.

470
00:41:26,616 --> 00:41:28,543
Scott: So how many total games can you get?

471
00:41:28,644 --> 00:41:33,040
Scott: If you, if a game is only 20 minutes, you can get a lot more games and a lot more people going through.

472
00:41:33,060 --> 00:41:47,886
Scott: the number of people that were able to play an RPG at an official session at Pax Unplugged was much smaller than the number of people who could play, you know, any kind of board game just by the nature of the beast.

473
00:41:48,849 --> 00:41:55,569
Scott: But yet clearly, if you look at the expo hall, RPGs are what people were trying to sell the most.

474
00:41:55,589 --> 00:42:02,596
Scott: I don't know if those people were buying the most, but it's like, Hey, the, uh, you know, there's, it's hard to match up, right?

475
00:42:02,657 --> 00:42:04,564
Scott: I feel like that you're providing on that.

476
00:42:04,685 --> 00:42:13,527
Rym: If the con has to expand, I would try to get a lot of space in the hotels for long running RPGs.

477
00:42:13,908 --> 00:42:16,274
Scott: I think that's the thing that should move to the hotels.

478
00:42:16,295 --> 00:42:21,490
Scott: They should get some mega ballrooms and make like a mega RPG land.

479
00:42:25,820 --> 00:42:29,389
Rym: Some hotels will have like a lot of small rooms that are hard to use for things and cons.

480
00:42:29,589 --> 00:42:32,599
Rym: Usually those are ideal for RPGs.

481
00:42:32,619 --> 00:42:34,886
Rym: You need relatively quiet, intimate spaces.

482
00:42:34,926 --> 00:42:41,165
Scott: You can have some premier RPGs run in like the little office-y spaces that they have in the hotels.

483
00:42:41,285 --> 00:42:42,910
Rym: I'd be way into that.

484
00:42:43,851 --> 00:42:48,592
Scott: But are you going to spend half a day of one convention on a, on a special RPG in a special room?

485
00:42:48,752 --> 00:42:52,427
Rym: I mean, uh, honestly, I probably would, depending on the RPG.

486
00:42:52,468 --> 00:43:01,180
Rym: Like I don't want to play a D and D at the con, but if there was someone running like inheritance for real or, uh, I don't know, a cool blades in the dark scenario, I'd be into that.

487
00:43:02,044 --> 00:43:03,671
Scott: It depends on how many hours it is, right?

488
00:43:03,691 --> 00:43:10,863
Scott: Cause like, you know, at this con, I actually felt like every time we go to a pecs, it's like, I'm so pecs that I know where to go.

489
00:43:10,883 --> 00:43:12,248
Scott: I know what everything's doing.

490
00:43:12,308 --> 00:43:13,232
Scott: I can see everything.

491
00:43:13,292 --> 00:43:21,502
Scott: I can check everything out because I had to do panels and Friday and because we had to, you know, eat and sleep like adults and we enforce both nights.

492
00:43:21,824 --> 00:43:24,795
Scott: So, and I enforced two out of three nights and is not four days.

493
00:43:24,856 --> 00:43:25,759
Scott: It's only three days.

494
00:43:25,779 --> 00:43:28,529
Scott: I actually didn't get to see all the pecs.

495
00:43:28,570 --> 00:43:30,398
Scott: There were rooms I didn't get to go to.

496
00:43:30,418 --> 00:43:32,547
Scott: I didn't go to the, my classic cardboard room.

497
00:43:32,567 --> 00:43:33,411
Scott: Just never went to it.

498
00:43:33,532 --> 00:43:36,311
Scott: Didn't get to see what was going on in there, even though I'm pretty sure I know what was going on.

499
00:43:36,351 --> 00:43:37,517
Scott: There was the same as the other years.

500
00:43:37,537 --> 00:43:39,688
Scott: I always like, I like to check it out.

501
00:43:39,929 --> 00:43:40,833
Scott: I didn't get to check it out.

502
00:43:41,135 --> 00:43:44,632
Scott: There was so much going on at the pecs unplugged that I actually couldn't see it all.

503
00:43:45,114 --> 00:43:47,462
Scott: It makes me want to not enforce.

504
00:43:47,864 --> 00:43:56,392
Rym: I'm going to wait cause I chose to enforce partly because I want to make sure pecs unplugged succeeds because it serves a purpose I deeply care about.

505
00:43:56,874 --> 00:43:58,601
Scott: That's also why I enforced, right?

506
00:43:58,681 --> 00:44:00,027
Scott: Is like, it was, it was a sacrifice of my own.

507
00:44:01,330 --> 00:44:04,203
Rym: Cause I'll say this, we were pretty short staffed in some key areas.

508
00:44:04,484 --> 00:44:05,046
Scott: Oh yeah.

509
00:44:05,127 --> 00:44:07,499
Rym: So I'll give you, this is a little bit of inside baseball.

510
00:44:07,519 --> 00:44:09,108
Rym: I don't think I'm stepping over any lines here.

511
00:44:09,169 --> 00:44:13,890
Rym: So when I used, I've mostly enforced the main theater and Scott mostly enforces tabletop.

512
00:44:13,990 --> 00:44:15,235
Rym: I think you've always enforced tabletop.

513
00:44:16,058 --> 00:44:22,771
Rym: Like a long time ago, I was like the deputy manager of live streaming for a while back when that department did something different for what it does now.

514
00:44:22,811 --> 00:44:26,766
Rym: But I do main theater typically like a paxies over the years.

515
00:44:26,926 --> 00:44:35,795
Rym: I've enforced night shift, which is concerts that fill up or are dense and loud or like acquisitions incorporated kind of events that like will fill up.

516
00:44:36,638 --> 00:44:41,073
Rym: Uh, we'd usually have like four people on each side of main, of main events.

517
00:44:41,635 --> 00:44:48,137
Rym: Once the show starts, keep an eye on the crowd, keeping people from going backstage, just being helpful, like keeping an eye on things.

518
00:44:49,020 --> 00:44:54,316
Rym: Uh, at unplugged, I was solely responsible for the entirety of house, right?

519
00:44:55,018 --> 00:44:57,966
Rym: Like the, the right half of main events.

520
00:44:58,447 --> 00:45:05,448
Rym: I just had to manage that whole thing all night by myself, which was fun, but it was a lot of work.

521
00:45:05,489 --> 00:45:06,271
Rym: I didn't take any breaks.

522
00:45:06,772 --> 00:45:24,809
Rym: And I think the con relied heavily on the fact that a lot of the enforcers at this PAX Unplugged are veterans of a lot of PAX is who know what's up and know how to run a PAX, which is a wide collection of both soft and hard skills that are not necessarily easy to teach.

523
00:45:25,811 --> 00:45:25,992
Scott: Yup.

524
00:45:26,092 --> 00:45:34,344
Scott: I can say that like usually at Easts and where I haven't enforced at West, but at Easts tabletop, we always got some new enforcers in there, right?

525
00:45:34,364 --> 00:45:35,550
Scott: You know, a few, whatever.

526
00:45:36,111 --> 00:45:36,693
Scott: at unplugged.

527
00:45:36,733 --> 00:45:40,142
Scott: No, every single person on, on my shift was old hat.

528
00:45:40,303 --> 00:45:40,804
Rym: Yup.

529
00:45:41,165 --> 00:45:45,360
Rym: Oh, my shift was an experienced enforcer who needed no help.

530
00:45:45,601 --> 00:45:51,364
Rym: I'd say it was 80% the same people I work with at East to the point that we didn't even have a meeting of like, who does what?

531
00:45:51,705 --> 00:45:56,229
Rym: we all just walked in and kind of went to their, went to the place we thought was right and just ran it.

532
00:45:56,269 --> 00:46:00,691
Rym: Like, we didn't really need, like we didn't have to plan anything because it was all like 10 year veterans.

533
00:46:02,254 --> 00:46:03,777
Rym: But that's, that's unsustainable.

534
00:46:03,817 --> 00:46:07,825
Rym: We need more enforcers.

535
00:46:08,707 --> 00:46:13,043
Rym: The main events was actually interesting because for the first time PAX Unplugged had concerts.

536
00:46:13,666 --> 00:46:15,777
Rym: And I remember thinking, what concerts would they put there?

537
00:46:15,797 --> 00:46:16,802
Rym: I could think of a few things.

538
00:46:17,305 --> 00:46:21,182
Rym: It was the old PAX, West standby, Jonathan Colton, Paul and storm.

539
00:46:21,222 --> 00:46:21,704
Rym: And you know what?

540
00:46:22,427 --> 00:46:26,497
Rym: To their credit, they kept like they filled that room.

541
00:46:27,360 --> 00:46:32,134
Rym: And I would say only about a fifth of the people in that room had any idea who the hell they were.

542
00:46:33,136 --> 00:46:40,744
Rym: Those people who knew who they were responded correctly to the call-outs because they did the same set they've done forever.

543
00:46:40,985 --> 00:46:41,768
Rym: Like no changes.

544
00:46:41,809 --> 00:46:43,454
Scott: They don't, it's been 20 years.

545
00:46:43,494 --> 00:46:44,237
Scott: They don't have new music.

546
00:46:44,257 --> 00:46:46,123
Rym: We are the opening band.

547
00:46:46,144 --> 00:46:49,935
Rym: It's literally like same everything, the pants song.

548
00:46:50,457 --> 00:46:55,191
Rym: But I would say four fifths of that room had no idea what this was going to be.

549
00:46:55,773 --> 00:46:57,378
Rym: And they were into it.

550
00:46:59,424 --> 00:46:59,986
Scott: I mean, yeah.

551
00:47:00,086 --> 00:47:01,913
Scott: You know, if you haven't seen it before, it works, right?

552
00:47:02,034 --> 00:47:04,769
Scott: It's like, that's how Broadway show goes on for years and years and years.

553
00:47:04,789 --> 00:47:05,834
Scott: It's like, yeah, this works.

554
00:47:06,316 --> 00:47:08,426
Scott: And people haven't seen it until they've seen it.

555
00:47:08,547 --> 00:47:09,872
Scott: It's you can't see it anywhere else.

556
00:47:09,892 --> 00:47:16,449
Scott: You know, so I would say likely to have seen Paul storm or Jonathan Colton, unless you already knew who they were and sought them out.

557
00:47:17,833 --> 00:47:23,269
Rym: So I would say having a concert at night at PAX Unplugged as an experiment was a huge success.

558
00:47:25,650 --> 00:47:28,684
Rym: Panels got a ton of attendance across the board as far as I can tell.

559
00:47:28,744 --> 00:47:30,833
Rym: Like our panel was at noon on Friday.

560
00:47:30,873 --> 00:47:35,411
Rym: It was like the first thing, like the story time was like still going or just wrapping up.

561
00:47:35,471 --> 00:47:41,933
Rym: Like it was, we were the first thing in that room and we were like a little of almost four fists of the way full.

562
00:47:42,194 --> 00:47:43,520
Rym: I'd say in that room, I got a photo.

563
00:47:43,921 --> 00:47:44,685
Scott: It's like a count.

564
00:47:45,488 --> 00:47:48,161
Scott: Hard to say, but it wasn't full, but it wasn't fun.

565
00:47:48,644 --> 00:47:54,890
Scott: But it was, there was, there was, you know, a healthy scattering of people all about the room, uh, in a room I didn't even know existed.

566
00:47:56,272 --> 00:47:57,640
Rym: I didn't know that this room existed either.

567
00:47:57,680 --> 00:48:01,216
Rym: I walk in, it's like an actual theater with theater seating in a real stage.

568
00:48:02,039 --> 00:48:02,200
Scott: Yeah.

569
00:48:02,220 --> 00:48:03,746
Scott: It's like, Whoa, what's that room doing here?

570
00:48:03,766 --> 00:48:04,247
Scott: Yeah.

571
00:48:04,609 --> 00:48:08,989
Scott: Um, I mean, you'll see it in the video, I guess eventually at least the stage part of it.

572
00:48:09,009 --> 00:48:09,129
Rym: Yep.

573
00:48:09,471 --> 00:48:16,380
Rym: But the panel content overall, like, you know, the thing that we seek out, there were panels I put on my schedule that I wanted to go to.

574
00:48:16,421 --> 00:48:20,056
Scott: that I just didn't go to cause I was busy, you know, eating.

575
00:48:20,718 --> 00:48:20,939
Rym: Yup.

576
00:48:21,340 --> 00:48:29,133
Rym: I want to do an Elizabeth Hargrave's a story time and I wish I couldn't cause we had to do our panel and I really wanted to go to that panel on the Kurt Vonnegut game, but we couldn't go to that.

577
00:48:29,154 --> 00:48:29,234
Rym: Nope.

578
00:48:30,881 --> 00:48:43,688
Rym: Everyone who went to it said it was amazing and exactly the kind of content we would have liked, but I'd say I'd characterize the content of the panels at unplugged was broadly closer to the kind of content I like at cons like this.

579
00:48:43,748 --> 00:48:50,373
Rym: It wasn't so much industry telling you something about like, Oh, we launched final fantasy 13 and here's some swag or whatever.

580
00:48:50,854 --> 00:48:57,954
Rym: It was more like here's nerds talking in depth about a tabletop topic, which is the kind of content I like.

581
00:48:57,974 --> 00:48:59,881
Scott: Yeah.

582
00:48:59,941 --> 00:49:04,497
Scott: I mean, anytime I went past the panel room, there were people, you know, lining up.

583
00:49:04,898 --> 00:49:07,587
Scott: So it was, I'm sure I know, I don't know all of them.

584
00:49:07,647 --> 00:49:10,260
Scott: Cause I, it's not like I could go around that much and investigate.

585
00:49:10,300 --> 00:49:13,814
Scott: It was just, you know, what I, from what I saw, that's how it was going.

586
00:49:13,895 --> 00:49:14,075
Scott: Yup.

587
00:49:14,477 --> 00:49:18,693
Rym: The learn and play room was apparently full, like the entire con that learn and play room.

588
00:49:19,075 --> 00:49:23,830
Rym: Like I think unplugged could have as many as four rooms like that.

589
00:49:24,172 --> 00:49:27,130
Rym: If they could somehow do it and they would all be full for the entire car.

590
00:49:28,352 --> 00:49:30,868
Scott: Yeah, they could have, I think a second learn and play room at least.

591
00:49:30,888 --> 00:49:33,782
Scott: It's just, you need people and you need games and you need enough.

592
00:49:33,883 --> 00:49:36,775
Scott: Like the limitation is actually not even the tables.

593
00:49:36,876 --> 00:49:38,863
Scott: It was the number of copies of the game.

594
00:49:38,883 --> 00:49:39,265
Scott: Yup.

595
00:49:39,425 --> 00:49:45,490
Rym: I'm going to paraphrase and enforcer in the room when we were setting up and we said, we don't need a copy on stage to teach.

596
00:49:45,630 --> 00:49:46,292
Rym: We have slides.

597
00:49:46,613 --> 00:49:50,245
Rym: He basically said, Oh, thank God I can see another table.

598
00:49:51,067 --> 00:49:52,995
Rym: Cause there was already a line outside.

599
00:49:53,517 --> 00:49:59,459
Scott: If you're not going to use that copy of the game to teach the game, then that means six more people can come in and play.

600
00:49:59,480 --> 00:49:59,540
Scott: Yup.

601
00:50:00,624 --> 00:50:05,742
Scott: So it's like, if there were just, you know, in the room only as, you know, it's like, what, how many copies did we have?

602
00:50:05,762 --> 00:50:10,738
Rym: Uh, we seated 72 people in the room for the workshop divided by six.

603
00:50:10,778 --> 00:50:12,824
Scott: is what, you know, 12, 12 counts.

604
00:50:12,864 --> 00:50:15,692
Scott: Yeah, I think.

605
00:50:16,014 --> 00:50:17,058
Rym: And they turn people away.

606
00:50:17,179 --> 00:50:21,576
Rym: Like, uh, I get the, it sounds like for almost all these learning plays, a lot of people got turned away,

607
00:50:22,399 --> 00:50:29,151
Scott: but it's like, we could have had 15 or 20 copies and that would have more people would have been able to play and come in and it would have filled right.

608
00:50:29,171 --> 00:50:30,074
Scott: there were enough people.

609
00:50:31,157 --> 00:50:33,363
Rym: So it's like, but I have two lessons there.

610
00:50:33,523 --> 00:50:39,903
Rym: One, I am super confident a learn and play room could exist at all.

611
00:50:39,923 --> 00:50:43,940
Rym: the other PAX is and similarly fill like, even though those PAX is not tabletop focus.

612
00:50:44,021 --> 00:50:53,117
Scott: I would also like to see the learn and play room, do some video games, but again, the limitation, the limitation will be controllers, consoles, screens, devices, copies of the game.

613
00:50:53,197 --> 00:50:59,337
Rym: Imagine learn and play quake to deathmatch with someone who's good at quake to deathmatch.

614
00:50:59,598 --> 00:51:01,849
Rym: And then you play quake to deathmatch in the room for an hour.

615
00:51:02,591 --> 00:51:11,521
Scott: Well, I think that this could, that could work really, really well specifically because they already have a densely packed PC area, right?

616
00:51:11,821 --> 00:51:15,733
Scott: So you could set up a PC land room with rows and rows of tables.

617
00:51:15,753 --> 00:51:16,777
Scott: They already do that.

618
00:51:17,058 --> 00:51:19,307
Scott: And that can get a high capacity of people.

619
00:51:19,347 --> 00:51:20,130
Scott: The game is on.

620
00:51:20,772 --> 00:51:23,702
Scott: all the equipment, all the software is already there and ready to go.

621
00:51:23,722 --> 00:51:30,567
Scott: And that would work best as opposed to say like learn and play smash brothers, which is a little less dense.

622
00:51:30,647 --> 00:51:38,049
Rym: But imagine like if there was a, Hey, learn and play how to be good at game cube smash brothers.

623
00:51:38,129 --> 00:51:40,296
Rym: Like I might just go to that.

624
00:51:40,317 --> 00:51:42,042
Rym: I think that would be fun.

625
00:51:42,143 --> 00:51:49,910
Scott: Even if I suck ass at something and I'm probably not even interested in playing it competitively or seriously or ever again at all.

626
00:51:50,572 --> 00:52:04,983
Scott: I would still, you know, it's different to what, but between watching a YouTube video and trying to copy it, if you actually have someone there, you know, cause there are some times I try to learn something like that from YouTube and I can, it doesn't help.

627
00:52:05,204 --> 00:52:14,072
Rym: I do learn some overwatch things from watching YouTube videos, but these are like intense, densely, some things you can and some things you can't.

628
00:52:14,132 --> 00:52:14,413
Scott: Right.

629
00:52:15,998 --> 00:52:18,746
Rym: But learn and play also like any convention.

630
00:52:18,786 --> 00:52:24,122
Rym: I think, I think there is a deep hunger for learn and play style content.

631
00:52:24,463 --> 00:52:25,466
Rym: Learning games is hard.

632
00:52:26,008 --> 00:52:30,143
Rym: Games are more fun to play when the people you play them with also know how to play them.

633
00:52:30,886 --> 00:52:34,938
Rym: And I think a lot of gaming groups rely heavily on their one friend who will teach everyone games.

634
00:52:36,182 --> 00:52:36,402
Scott: Yep.

635
00:52:36,623 --> 00:52:36,723
Scott: Yep.

636
00:52:36,743 --> 00:52:42,200
Rym: Despite the crowds, like nothing was too agro.

637
00:52:42,220 --> 00:52:43,765
Rym: The crowds were super well behaved.

638
00:52:44,387 --> 00:52:47,058
Rym: It was very dense, but it wasn't really a problem.

639
00:52:47,078 --> 00:52:47,801
Rym: Yeah.

640
00:52:47,861 --> 00:52:56,553
Scott: It was dense, but like you could make your way through other than of course, people walking slowly in front of me, but that's every con ever even ones that aren't so crowded.

641
00:52:57,397 --> 00:53:04,786
Rym: It did take me almost 20 minutes just to walk over to the info booth and back from where we were table-topping just because the crowd was going so slowly.

642
00:53:04,966 --> 00:53:05,207
Scott: Right.

643
00:53:05,227 --> 00:53:11,856
Scott: I mean, it's still annoying that the entering and exiting the building, there's like only two security entrances or whatever.

644
00:53:11,937 --> 00:53:14,670
Scott: And it's like you have to, you can only enter at like the ends of the con.

645
00:53:14,770 --> 00:53:17,848
Scott: You can't enter in the middle, which is be the most convenient place to enter.

646
00:53:19,871 --> 00:53:22,784
Scott: And they strictly limit like these are exits only.

647
00:53:22,844 --> 00:53:24,010
Scott: These are entrances only.

648
00:53:25,611 --> 00:53:26,594
Scott: That's all that's annoying.

649
00:53:27,757 --> 00:53:31,188
Rym: But I mean, overall PAX Unplugged, like.

650
00:53:31,228 --> 00:53:36,466
Rym: I feel like we won't need to do future reviews of PAX Unplugged unless something material changes.

651
00:53:36,546 --> 00:53:41,000
Rym: But I'd say as of now PAX Unplugged is a great PAX among taxes.

652
00:53:41,020 --> 00:53:42,284
Rym: I think it is now.

653
00:53:42,304 --> 00:53:45,737
Rym: I'm not going to count PAX Australia cause I haven't been doing so long.

654
00:53:45,798 --> 00:53:47,606
Rym: I can't like put it in consideration.

655
00:53:48,069 --> 00:53:49,294
Rym: It's my second favorite PAX.

656
00:53:49,334 --> 00:53:58,310
Rym: The only PAX I like more is West partly just because PAX West convention center remains the best convention center for a nerd convention I have ever physically entered.

657
00:54:02,012 --> 00:54:03,256
Rym: Name a better one you've entered.

658
00:54:04,219 --> 00:54:04,681
Scott: I've entered.

659
00:54:04,721 --> 00:54:06,487
Scott: I mean, you know, it's good.

660
00:54:06,667 --> 00:54:10,479
Rym: It's like wrong with I'm saying name a better one that you've been inside of.

661
00:54:11,302 --> 00:54:21,038
Scott: I guess I was very specific, but it's not, it's not so good that it's like, you know, greatly improving the con and making it magical or anything.

662
00:54:21,118 --> 00:54:23,210
Rym: I think it is greatly improving the con in one regard.

663
00:54:23,310 --> 00:54:26,661
Rym: The fact that everything except the expo hall is carpeted is a big deal.

664
00:54:26,681 --> 00:54:34,268
Rym: Imagine PAX Unplugged with no changes, except tabletop free play and the main floor was also carpeted.

665
00:54:37,811 --> 00:54:39,738
Rym: So, uh, yeah, so we'll be back.

666
00:54:39,939 --> 00:54:42,067
Rym: I, if you've skipped out in fact, good luck.

667
00:54:42,247 --> 00:54:44,016
Scott: Good luck trying to go to PAX Unplugged.

668
00:54:44,056 --> 00:54:52,033
Scott: They've already announced all the dates for all the PAX is next year and the theme for next year, which is some sort of like subway street art looking style, which seems okay to me.

669
00:54:52,153 --> 00:55:06,710
Rym: I think we're entering an era again, like the heady days of 2010, 2011 where every pack sells out and, and magfest and just, I don't think the East and the West are going to sell out until the video game industry changes.

670
00:55:07,050 --> 00:55:08,057
Rym: Actually, no, I take that back.

671
00:55:08,158 --> 00:55:09,850
Rym: I think West might start selling out again.

672
00:55:10,410 --> 00:55:14,283
Rym: I don't think East will, and I'm not sure West will.

673
00:55:14,805 --> 00:55:17,857
Rym: Unplugged will definitely sell out every year forever from now.

674
00:55:18,318 --> 00:55:21,130
Scott: Unplugged is good is until, until something changes.

675
00:55:24,692 --> 00:55:29,889
Rym: Like magfest is limited basically primarily by the fact that there's no hotels anywhere near it.

676
00:55:31,111 --> 00:55:36,334
Scott: But yeah, uh, I'm not so confident the video game industry is going to recover that quickly.

677
00:55:36,354 --> 00:55:44,189
Scott: Uh, and I'm not going to say the tabletop will, will fall, but I'm not going to say that it's going to hold and keep growing forever.

678
00:55:44,710 --> 00:55:45,392
Scott: It's good.

679
00:55:45,493 --> 00:55:46,556
Scott: Eventually there's

680
00:55:46,596 --> 00:56:06,322
Rym: a difference here is that if the video game industry falls, video game conventions all follow along with them for a lot of reasons, but tabletop, the demand from people who want tabletop conventions and want to play games is so much greater than even the money the industry makes.

681
00:56:06,964 --> 00:56:12,330
Rym: that the community demand can almost make conventions happen even if the industry itself is not helping.

682
00:56:12,350 --> 00:56:17,192
Scott: But then the badge price would have to make up for all the money they make from the expo hall instead.

683
00:56:17,212 --> 00:56:22,030
Scott: And that would, that badge price might become prohibitive for the large tabletop.

684
00:56:22,151 --> 00:56:29,509
Rym: While it might, my gut feeling, and this is pure gut feeling, if you doubled the price of a Pax Unplugged badge, I think it would still sell out.

685
00:56:30,650 --> 00:56:39,630
Scott: Maybe if, but if there's no expo hall there because the industry flopped and people just want to play games that already exist, would people would be willing to pay twice as much for the con?

686
00:56:40,973 --> 00:56:41,776
Rym: They actually might.

687
00:56:41,796 --> 00:56:47,475
Rym: I think just for the space, partly because the culture of Pax Unplugged, just polling attendees.

688
00:56:47,496 --> 00:56:50,247
Rym: So again, sample size of Rym talk to like eight or nine people.

689
00:56:50,287 --> 00:56:51,894
Rym: So don't, read too much into this.

690
00:56:52,698 --> 00:56:57,842
Rym: I really get the impression a lot of the people who were at Pax Unplugged were there for the whole weekend.

691
00:56:58,606 --> 00:56:59,490
Rym: Like I'm here.

692
00:56:59,571 --> 00:57:02,270
Scott: I do think there were more people who were there multiple days.

693
00:57:02,670 --> 00:57:08,368
Rym: When Pax started, the original Paxes, they only sold badges for the whole weekend.

694
00:57:08,448 --> 00:57:13,021
Rym: And old Pax West, only all weekend people were coming for the most part.

695
00:57:13,362 --> 00:57:15,308
Rym: Pax East changed that.

696
00:57:15,749 --> 00:57:19,301
Rym: That was when they shifted to selling individual day badges.

697
00:57:19,321 --> 00:57:24,901
Rym: And I think that was the moment where Pax East suddenly like at night it cleared out.

698
00:57:24,962 --> 00:57:31,525
Rym: because suddenly a huge percentage of the people who go to Pax East only come for one day and are primarily expo hall people.

699
00:57:31,986 --> 00:57:38,050
Rym: Unplugged has resurfaced the old Pax culture of you come for the whole weekend.

700
00:57:40,990 --> 00:57:41,131
Scott: Indeed.

701
00:57:41,212 --> 00:57:45,294
Scott: Well, I mean, if you're, you know, you're looking to play games one day of games is not enough, right?

702
00:57:45,595 --> 00:57:49,269
Scott: Also, you know, if you're coming to Unplugged, they're coming to play games.

703
00:57:49,330 --> 00:57:52,343
Scott: If you go to East or West, people might not be coming to play games.

704
00:57:52,484 --> 00:57:56,480
Rym: My train home from Pax, hold on, the station in Philadelphia.

705
00:57:56,500 --> 00:58:02,525
Scott: Again, why would you, why would you pay money and travel and hotel nights to play video games you could play at home?

706
00:58:02,826 --> 00:58:05,877
Scott: Why would you pay money, travel and hotel nights to play board games?

707
00:58:06,840 --> 00:58:07,764
Scott: Oh, that makes sense.

708
00:58:08,065 --> 00:58:08,225
Rym: Yep.

709
00:58:08,506 --> 00:58:09,188
Scott: That's right.

710
00:58:09,229 --> 00:58:09,831
Scott: To play an RP.

711
00:58:09,951 --> 00:58:12,360
Scott: Oh, that makes sense to do that.

712
00:58:12,480 --> 00:58:17,738
Rym: But my train home, which was a different train from Scott's train home completely sold out.

713
00:58:18,119 --> 00:58:26,825
Rym: I'd say 80% of the people on that train were Pax attendees and a card game and a bunch of like switch gaming was happening.

714
00:58:27,066 --> 00:58:29,192
Rym: That train car was really rowdy the whole way home.

715
00:58:29,232 --> 00:58:33,024
Scott: My train car home was also full of Pax people.

716
00:58:33,044 --> 00:58:36,919
Scott: And well, I love all those Pax people and they were lovely Pax people.

717
00:58:37,883 --> 00:58:39,389
Scott: It's fucking like 10 o'clock at night.

718
00:58:39,429 --> 00:58:40,613
Scott: I'm gonna take it a train home to New York.

719
00:58:40,633 --> 00:58:41,075
Scott: Shut up.

720
00:58:42,138 --> 00:58:42,860
Scott: Jesus Christ.

721
00:58:42,880 --> 00:58:43,362
Scott: I'm old.

722
00:58:43,763 --> 00:58:44,385
Scott: Be quiet.

723
00:58:44,927 --> 00:58:47,818
Scott: Stop talking games and all the stuff you did at Pax so badly.

724
00:58:47,838 --> 00:58:49,506
Scott: There's only one quiet car.

725
00:58:49,526 --> 00:58:50,290
Scott: I couldn't get in there.

726
00:58:51,750 --> 00:58:53,455
Scott: They can't make all the cars quiet cars.

727
00:58:53,595 --> 00:58:55,621
Scott: You can talk just quieter.

728
00:58:55,641 --> 00:58:57,205
Scott: I'm old.

729
00:58:58,068 --> 00:58:58,329
Rym: Yeah.

730
00:58:58,569 --> 00:59:00,740
Rym: Younger, younger Rym would have been more into that.

731
00:59:00,760 --> 00:59:02,248
Rym: And that's why I am tolerant of it.

732
00:59:02,348 --> 00:59:05,904
Rym: because uh, the old man should step aside.

733
00:59:06,045 --> 00:59:07,089
Rym: Let the young man have fun.

734
00:59:08,151 --> 00:59:09,096
Scott: They weren't that much younger.

735
00:59:09,116 --> 00:59:10,504
Scott: These are, you know, twenties thirties.

736
00:59:10,604 --> 00:59:20,299
Rym: That's a lot younger in terms of when I was in my twenties, they announced that Pax will no longer go until 2am and will in fact end at a cowardly midnight.

737
00:59:20,700 --> 00:59:22,365
Rym: And I remember being mad about that.

738
00:59:22,365 --> 00:59:29,684
Rym: 42 year old Rym could not possibly make it to 2am out of Pax.

739
00:59:31,128 --> 00:59:31,630
Scott: No, no chance.

740
00:59:35,592 --> 00:59:37,961
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

741
00:59:38,021 --> 00:59:40,591
Rym: Special thanks to DJ Pretzel for the opening theme.

742
00:59:40,732 --> 00:59:46,795
Scott: Be sure to visit our website at frontrowclue.com for show notes, discussion, links, and more.

743
00:59:47,237 --> 00:59:49,890
Rym: Remember, GeekNights is not one, but four different shows.

744
00:59:50,250 --> 00:59:51,877
Rym: Mondays are science and technology.

745
00:59:51,917 --> 00:59:53,685
Rym: Tuesdays gaming and games.

746
00:59:53,805 --> 00:59:55,613
Rym: Wednesdays are animation and comics.

747
00:59:55,934 --> 00:59:58,464
Rym: Thursdays are arbitrary and indiscriminate.

748
00:59:59,067 --> 01:00:04,044
Rym: GeekNights is distributed under a creative comments attribution 4.0 international license.

749
01:00:04,446 --> 01:00:12,732
Scott: GeekNights is recorded live with no studio and no audience, but unlike those other late shows, it's actually recorded at night.

750
01:00:13,355 --> 01:00:24,417
Rym: And the Patreon patrons for this episode of GeekNights are Alan Jones and PG Dredd, Lily Tenebrae, K-Spare, Mix of Music, Krista Voorhe, Clinton, Walt, and Finn, Joel Hayes, Penny Rhymer, Rebecca Adams, Sam Markey, Sandra Van Holl, and a bunch of people who do not want me to say their names.

751
01:00:24,659 --> 01:00:25,844
Rym: Yeah, PAX Unplugged was great.

752
01:00:25,965 --> 01:00:28,995
Rym: And for any of you who are wondering, zero trouble with the union.

753
01:00:29,457 --> 01:00:32,690
Rym: One, the union guys were super cool and kind of into the content.

754
01:00:33,730 --> 01:00:50,892
Rym: And two, the rules of the convention center, I actually learned them specifically, are you do not need to hire a camera operator, even if you have a tripod, as long as you do not plug into a power outlet and do not touch the camera or the tripod at any point during the event you're recording.

755
01:00:51,374 --> 01:00:53,803
Rym: So we set up multiple cameras, no problem.

756
01:00:54,165 --> 01:00:57,037
Rym: I'll try to have the panel up like this weekend at the latest.

757
01:00:57,579 --> 01:00:58,785
Rym: So yeah, it was all a good time.

758
01:00:58,905 --> 01:01:00,651
Rym: I hope to see you all at future PAX Unplugged.

759
01:01:01,031 --> 01:01:10,240
Rym: And tonight I will leave you with... Hey Mario, look what I made.

760
01:01:10,840 --> 01:01:12,285
Rym: It's a stone, Luigi.

761
01:01:12,586 --> 01:01:13,990
Rym: You didn't make it.

762
01:01:15,535 --> 01:01:16,397
Rym: It's a football.

763
01:01:16,678 --> 01:01:17,260
Rym: I chiseled it.

764
01:01:17,780 --> 01:01:19,287
Rym: Well, what are you waiting for?

765
01:01:19,668 --> 01:01:20,693
Scott: Throw me a pass.

766
01:01:21,095 --> 01:01:22,240
Rym: It's even official size.

767
01:01:24,169 --> 01:01:25,520
Scott: But not official weight.

